<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr Atiku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Abubakar Atiku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Bakr Atiku was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>, ruling from 1837 to 1842. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, the founder of the caliphate, he succeeded his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello</a> and was known for his strict religious policy. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umaru-nagwamatse">Umaru Nagwamatse</a> later founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kontagora">Kontagora</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, and another son, Abubakar II Atiku na Raba, also became caliph.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Bakr Atiku was born in 1782 to the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>. Like all his siblings he studied under his father's direction at Degel until the family and some of its followers went into exile in 1804. Like his brother Muhammed Bello and his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nana-asmau">Nana Asma'u</a>, he gave much of his early life to study and wrote poetry, history and works of Islamic learning, many of which survive and are counted among the key texts of the era.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a> died in 1815, Atiku briefly contested the succession with Bello. The struggle was bloodless: Bello became sultan, and Atiku was imprisoned for a year, after which he served as his brother's adviser for the rest of Bello's life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Atiku succeeded Bello in October 1837. Some of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> disliked him and worked for other candidates, but he was quickly named sultan. He set out to reverse policies of Bello's that he thought extravagant, banning dancing and music and making religious instruction more rigid, and he moved the seat of government back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> from Wurno, where Bello had ruled in his last years.</p><p>His reign was unpopular with both the people and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>. The tension came to a head over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adamawa-emirate">Adamawa Emirate</a>, whose ruler Modibo Adama had fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>'s war. Atiku recognised an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> under Hamman Sambo, a ruler he thought more loyal, which took a large part of Adamawa's territory. Adama threatened to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and turn to the Ottoman Caliphate, and Adamawa came close to civil war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the autumn of 1842 Atiku led an attack on Tsibiri in Maradun, with the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> said to be present. He was wounded and, already weakened by illness and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> fast, died at Katuru. His successor quickly withdrew the recognition of Hamman Sambo. Two of his sons, Ahmadu Atiku and Abd ar-Rahman dan Abi Bakar, later became sultans, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-attahiru-i">Muhammadu Attahiru I</a> was the last sultan of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Bakr Atiku” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kontagora was founded around 1859 by Umaru Nagwamatse, a son of the Sokoto caliph <b>Abubakar Atiku</b> and grandson of Usman dan Fodio.</blockquote><a href="/state/kontagora-emirate">Kontagora Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Sokoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.06,5.23&amp;year=1832" aria-label="Sokoto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4694 1677 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Abu Bakr Atiku"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5144 1947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5144 1947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=13.06,5.23&amp;year=1832">What was here in 1832? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
